A||||6||Roy||Things that should be replaced||||1||
Z||000000||Roy||05-19-2000||03:02 PM||||Ok I got all the lumber I need to make the craddle to drop the engine craddle onto and the 4x4 to raise it with two jacks with. I just wanna know, what else I should change while I have the engine out. Its a v6 with 90k on it. I figure I'll do all the seals just to be safe, anything else you guys can think of that is most likely going to fail soon or would be easier to replace with the engine out. Also I wanna put a new intake on it mine has a leak on it. My bolts are rusted in place how would I go about getting them out. <P>Roy<P>(btw this car is a 88gt if that helps at all)||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||05-19-2000||03:09 PM||||I would replace any sensors or coolant hoses that look crusty.  That's what I'm doing with mine.  Might also want to consider having fuel injectors tested, or at least install new o-rings on them...  Might as well have heads and exhaust manifolds ported too!||||reg||1
Z||000002||LarryB||05-19-2000||04:12 PM||||at 90K and a 12 yr old car I'd probably replace the water pump and radiator hoses, maybe the starter. If its a 5 speed and you don't know for sure when the clutch was replaced I'd look into replacing that as well.  All of those eventually let go and would probably be easier to replace with the cradle out of the car.<P>look at the engine mounts, the exhaust system including manifolds, the intake manifolds, and the rear struts to see if anything needs repair or replacement.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||05-19-2000||05:29 PM||||I just put the front wheels on cinder bocks with a piece of 4*4 in the hole and jacked up the rear end and put jack stands under the body near the front of the rear wheel wheels. I put a piece of wood between the jack stand and the car body to distribute the weight evenly over that area. Then I dropped the whole cradle assembly by lowering it with a good hydraulic jack and rolled it out with that. It worked like a charm and I didn't have to buy any wood or dismantle the rear of the body.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||05-20-2000||05:29 PM||||Freeze plugs. All of them. I'm not sure if the 2.8 has any on the back in the bell housing or not. (They sometimes hide 1 or 2 behind the flywheel.) If it does I'd definatly replace them.<P>While the fly wheel is off replace the rear main seal.<P>you're going this far pull the cover and put in a new timing set and front crank seal.||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||05-20-2000||08:49 PM||||And while you're at it, rebuild the engine, replace the exhuast, and lubricate your Johnson Rods.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phaeton||05-21-2000||12:37 AM||||If your hands are large now is the time to change spark plugs.<BR>I've rebuilt the calipers, put brushes in the starter, changed the clutch, but I CANNOT change the front plugs without dropping the engine. Arrrrgh!||||reg||1
